Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:34:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] docs: cmdbus-writer tier service, the engine lock, and the
 probe gap

Adds cmdbus-writer-tier-service, recording two facts about the writer role
(NOETL_COMMAND_BUS_HOST / NOETL_EVENT_BUS_HOST) established during the
2026-09-22 prod diagnosis:

- :9102/metrics is not a counter read. It samples the durability window and
  takes the engine lock, so a slow tier op blocks the scrape and KEDA reports
  it as an unreachable endpoint. The same lock sits in the server's command-
  and event-bus path, so a tier stall reaches request paths that never touch
  the bus. Measured: read_execution 5.587s mean with a ~36.4s tail, one 75.9s
  scan, against health at 0.376ms in the same process; 16.6% of tier
  connections shed or failed against max_inflight=4.
- The deployed writer probes are TCP-only for liveness AND readiness, on
  :9101 rather than any face that stalls, so the pod stays Ready with zero
  restarts through a stall of any length. That is why an 18-hour degradation
  alerted nobody.

Also notes that ehdb_feed_subject_lag is per-pool: commands.shared sat at 0
for a day while commands.system climbed past 600, so reading the total or the
wrong subject attributes a backlog to the wrong deployment.

Updates deployment-specification (9102 ports row, a writer-probe subsection
under Health probes) and cross-links from Home and _Sidebar.

Refs noetl/ai-meta#351
Refs noetl/ai-meta#322

